prop councilgate if.use=signal|councildoor
prop councilgate else.use=message||The council door has no key slot here: it opens from the keypad beside it.
note bridgecrate Bridgeworks crane's toolbox | The crane that built the Crown's bridges lies where the storm of '31 dropped it. Its toolbox survived.
loot bridgecrate MetalIngot*3;Bolt*4;Nail*10
note rec_pip Pip's recording | A wind-up message box, painted blue, with a dent.
loot rec_pip story:plea*1
prop rec_pip loot.refill=0
prop rec_pip on.open=message||Pip's voice, very close to the box: Hello. This is Pip Penn, from the Rookery. Nan says I have to say it properly. We are not weight. We are people. Nan is a person and I am a person and my dad was a person and he drove the tram. That's all. Nan, is that enough? (Maud, further off: That's plenty, love.) Oh. Also please give Rook back his water, I want my tram.
note rec_crane Crane's broadcast | The Authority's broadcast set, its last recording still on the spool.
loot rec_crane story:plea*1
prop rec_crane loot.refill=0
prop rec_crane on.open=message||Councillor Crane, calm and reasonable over the rain: People of Highwater. Tonight the council will settle the water. Some of you will be told stories about valves and ledgers. Ask yourselves who tells them. The city has stood for ninety years because someone did the arithmetic. Not one pound more than it can bear. I have carried every pound of it for you. I ask only that you let me finish.
note rec_teo Teo's recording | A ship's message cylinder, wedged under the Fleet's flag.
loot rec_teo story:plea*1
prop rec_teo loot.refill=0
prop rec_teo on.open=message||Teo Varga, hoarse: Stranger. I've heard what you're carrying. I'll say it before the council so nobody can say it for me: the Fleet took Crane's money after Lowstreet. Fuel money, for forty families. I knew the name on that valve log wasn't mine and I kept my mouth shut and my boats fed. If you read the log aloud tonight, read my name too. I'd rather be ashamed than be quiet.
note rec_bram Bram's recording | A Union whistle-box, the kind the cranes use for shift calls.
loot rec_bram story:plea*1
prop rec_bram loot.refill=0
prop rec_bram on.open=message||Bram Hask, with crane noise behind him: Eleven thousand tons. You've seen the book. The Union took Crane's money for the widows' fund, and I told myself that made it clean. It didn't. Ines - if you're listening, girl - your hook's still on the wall. Stranger: don't choose us because we're honest. We're not. Choose whatever keeps the most people dry. And then somebody tell me what that man is building at the bottom of the sea.
note rec_nell Nell's recording | A clerk's dictation cylinder, labelled in neat capitals: N. ABERNETHY.
loot rec_nell story:plea*1
prop rec_nell loot.refill=0
prop rec_nell on.open=message||Nell Abernethy, quickly and quietly: I'm the night clerk. I've been reassigned to the Crown so he can watch me, which means I've watched him. Every night this week he has gone down the pile stair alone with a lantern and come back soaked to the waist. He's stopped sleeping. He talks to the jacks. Whatever you choose in there, someone has to go down after him, because I think the piles are worse than he has told even himself.
note dobbs400 Complaint No. 400 | COMPLAINT No. 400\nTo: the Harbor Council, in session, in the storm, which is where I have been told I may finally be heard.\n\nI am Wilmer Dobbs. I have written three hundred and ninety-nine complaints and the Councillor has filed every one of them in a drawer with a ribbon round it. I have seen the ribbon. It is a nice ribbon. It does not stop pumps.\n\nI complain that the Lowstreet main was run backwards for nine years on purpose. I complain that the Rookery was weighed. I complain that the Council's barges went to the Piles low in the water and came back light.\n\nAnd I complain - this is the new one - that the Councillor went down the pile stair an hour ago with a lantern and has not come up.\n\nYours, as ever, and I mean it this time, W. Dobbs
note councilnotebook Councillor's notebook | Crane's notebook.\n\nThe council door: I gave each faction one piece so no faction can open it alone.\nThe Authority has the first, the fleet the middle two, the union the last. I keep the whole in the cave, in case I forget.
note strongbox Councillor's strongbox | The councillor's strongbox, locked with an Authority keycard: what he was paid to sign.
loot strongbox TradeToken*5;CircuitBoard*1;Glass*2
prop strongbox loot.refill=0
note cipherchest Councillor's cipher | Crane's hand again, on oilcloth:\n\nCOUNCIL DOOR - 4 7 1 9\n\nDon't tell the factions it was this easy.
loot cipherchest Battery*1;CopperIngot*2;Rope*2
prop cipherchest loot.refill=0
note cablechest Linesman's chest | A linesman's chest in the middle of the live cable bed. He must have known where the breaker was.
loot cablechest CopperIngot*3;CircuitBoard*1;Battery*1
prop cablechest loot.refill=0
quest title=The Council in the Storm
quest intro=The councils meet on the Crown tonight, in the storm - and Crane will be in the chair. You carry the valve log, the ledger page and Dobbs's complaints. Make for the four council roofs on the Crown and go up onto the landing roof.
step reach|landing_roof|1|Make for the four council roofs and go up onto the landing roof
step read|Storm bulletin|1|Read the storm bulletin
step kill|Screecher|4|Drive the four screechers off the roofs
step collect|story:shutters|1|Crank the landing roof's storm shutters shut
step reach|authority_roof|1|Cross to the Authority's roof
step read|Authority demand|1|Read the Authority's demand
step reach|fleet_roof|1|Cross to the Fleet's roof
step read|Fleet demand|1|Read the Fleet's demand
step reach|union_roof|1|Cross to the Union's roof
step read|Union demand|1|Read the Union's demand
step collect|story:plea|5|Play the five recordings left for you on the four roofs
step read|Complaint No. 400|1|Read the complaint nailed up on the key by the pile stair
step open|Faction letters|1|Open the factions' letters on the Union's roof
step read|Authority secret|1|Read the Authority's secret
step read|Fleet secret|1|Read the Fleet's secret
step read|Union secret|1|Read the Union's secret
step reach|storm_ridge|1|Climb storm ridge, the long ridge behind the council roofs
step read|Councillor's notebook|1|Read the councillor's notebook in his ruined summer house inland
step open|Bridgeworks crane's toolbox|1|Open the toolbox crate of the fallen bridgeworks crane on the shore
step read|Storm warden's notice|1|Read the storm warden's notice near the foot of Crown Fell
step reach|bc_trail|1|Find the foot of the trail up Crown Fell, the tall fell with the storm mast
step reach|bc_summit|1|Climb the trail to the top of Crown Fell
step collect|story:bc_top|1|Climb the storm beacon mast and light the beacon from its deck box
step collect|story:alarm|1|Ring the alarm bell on the Authority's roof - the council sits
step reach|council_door|1|Go to the council house door
step collect|story:councilcode|1|Open the door with the code the three letters make
step reach|council|1|Go into the council house
step read|Five ways|1|Read the five ways the city can go
step collect|story:choice|1|Pull one lever - choose for Highwater
step read|The verdict|1|Read the verdict
step open|Council reward|1|Open the council's reward
step read|Teo Varga's message|1|Read Teo Varga's message
step reach|pile_stair|1|Go down to the pile stair on the key's shore
step read|Pile engineer's map|1|Read the pile engineer's map
step reach|pile_head|1|Dive to the first pile head, where Crane's lantern went down
quest done=The council is over. Under the city, the piles are cracking - the foundations, band 400.
quest reward=TitaniumOre*2;TradeToken*6

